BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Measurement on the Color Line
DTSTART:20261016T000000
DTEND:20270815T000000
DTSTAMP:20261010T163808Z
UID:4f201f855403dd87f4055a7c925c55@events-near-me
DESCRIPTION:"Everyone thinks they know what race is—but do they?" Ideas 
 about racial categories and racial differences have been created and susta
 ined through scientific\, biological\, anthropological\, and medical pract
 ices. Measurement on the Color Line traces the development of present-day 
 notions of race through the sciences\, measurements\, and data that produc
 ed them. From the eighteenth century to the present\, scientific instrumen
 ts have been used in studies that make claims about race and racial differ
 ence. Specific categories of people have been formed through acts of obser
 vation\, measurement\, and quantification. New sciences and technologies h
 ave been repeatedly applied to answer familiar questions about race. Since
  the end of the nineteenth century\, some physicians and social scientists
  have offered a counter-narrative to challenge race-making measurements. C
 urated by Evelynn M. Hammonds\, the Barbara Gutmann Rosenkrantz Professor 
 of the History of Science and professor of African and African American St
 udies at Harvard\, the exhibition draws directly from her co-authored book
 \, with Bates College Professor Rebecca Herzing\, The Nature of Difference
 : Sciences of Race in the United States from Jefferson to Genomics. Her re
 search highlights how early scientific disciplines\, including anthropolog
 y and medicine\, sought physical justifications for social hierarchies. Me
 asurement on the Color Line includes objects graciously lent from several 
 institutions and individuals\, including the Peabody Museum of Archaeology
  & Ethnology\, the Dittrick Museum of Medical History at Case Western Rese
 rve University\, Archives and Special Collections at Amherst College\, Ern
 st Mayr Library\, Harvard Divinity School Library\, Harvard Law School Lib
 rary\, Houghton Library\, and artists Lucy Kim and Wendel White. LEARN MOR
 E Sources: Meet Boston Event page: https://www.meetboston.com/event/measur
 ement-on-the-color-line/93637/\n\nEvents Near Me: within 30 miles of North
 borough\, MA 01532\nDiscovery: Local fallback from massachusetts-events.ic
 s\nSource page: https://www.meetboston.com/event/measurement-on-the-color-
 line/93637/
LOCATION:Collection of Historical Scientific Instruments\, Special Exhibit
 ions Gallery\, Collection of Historical Scientific Instruments\, Special E
 xhibitions Gallery 1 Oxford Street\, Cambridge\, MA\, 02138
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/measurement-on-the-color-line/93637/
END:VEVENT
